## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to model DAG-driven work in Kubernetes and coordinate cross-namespace scheduling with a centralized DAG, while handling fairness and observability.

## Key Concepts
- DAG-based task orchestration across namespaces
- CRD and controller/operator design
- Job lifecycle, retries, backoff
- SchedulerPlugin for cross-tenant fairness
- Observability and safe cleanup

## Code Example
```javascript
// Skeleton reconcile loop for ComputeDAG tasks
async function reconcileComputeDAG(dag) {
  const ready = dag.tasks.filter(t => t.deps.every(d => d.status === 'Complete'));
  for (const t of ready) {
    if (!t.started) spawnJob(t);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you detect and break cycles in the DAG at reconciliation time?
- How would you test resilience to partial failures and ensure safe rollbacks across namespaces?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests multi-region disaster recovery patterns, CRD-driven control loops, data consistency trade-offs, and practical implementation experience in production Kubernetes environments.

## Key Concepts
- Kubernetes CustomResourceDefinition and controller patterns
- StatefulSet management across multiple regions
- Global DNS management and traffic steering
- Data consistency models and replication lag handling
- Leader election and coordination mechanisms
- Observability through metrics, events, and SLOs
- Safe failover sequencing with rollback capabilities
- Health-based promotion policies and decision making

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo reconciliation skeleton for GlobalDeployment
async function Reconcile(req) {
  const gd = await fetchGlobalDeployment(req);
  const primary = await getStatefulSet(gd.spec.primaryRegion);
  const standby = await getStatefulSet(gd.spec.standbyRegion);

  // Check health and sync status
  const primaryHealth = await checkRegionHealth(gd.spec.primaryRegion);
  const replicationLag = await measureReplicationLag(primary, standby);

  if (!primaryHealth.healthy && shouldPromote(gd, replicationLag)) {
    await initiateFailover(gd, primaryHealth, replicationLag);
  }

  // Update DNS if needed
  if (gd.status.activeRegion !== gd.spec.primaryRegion) {
    await updateDNS(gd.spec.dnsName, gd.status.activeRegion);
  }

  // Emit observability data
  emitMetrics(gd, primaryHealth, replicationLag);
  recordEvents(gd, 'reconciliation_complete');
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a Kubernetes-native control plane for GPU-backed ML workloads, balancing quotas, preemption, and data access while preserving isolation and observability.

## Key Concepts

- Kubernetes Operators and CRDs
- GPU scheduling with taints, tolerations, and resource requests
- Priority, preemption and multi-tenant quotas
- Data locality and streaming of dataset shards
- Observability, auditing, and lifecycle finalizers

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo reconciler loop for GpuTrainJob
while (true) {
  const jobs = listGpuTrainJobs();
  for (const j of jobs) {
    if (!quotaOk(j)) continue;
    if (!podExists(j)) {
      createGpuPod(j); // with nodeSelector for j.gpuType and resources
    }
    updateStatus(j, {phase: "Running"});
  }
  await sleep(1000);
}
```

